{"authored" [{"question" "q-91160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -8 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 18 & 0 \\\\ -13 & 28 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/state.json" "file_sha256" "39ca3612cc1476f345b5b0c22b9df36ae735185c4d19e174735bd47ec7b3faeb" "basis" 1412 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-91160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries below ${a}_{13}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -8 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-5{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 18 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -8 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-9{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 18 & 0 \\\\ -13 & 28 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-5{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-9{R}_{1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/state.json" "file_sha256" "39ca3612cc1476f345b5b0c22b9df36ae735185c4d19e174735bd47ec7b3faeb" "basis" 1412 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/edb-import/verification.json"} {"que