we need to make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{12}=5$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -7 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -8 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 8 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+{C}_{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/state.json" "file_sha256" "39ca3612cc1476f345b5b0c22b9df36ae735185c4d19e174735bd47ec7b3faeb" "basis" 1412 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-91189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9621d434515595164b9a7b82a0b62acb60abf05f431523c0f0f85bd07935f033" "basis" 1412 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-91189" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/state.json" "file_sha256" "39ca3612cc1476f345b5b0c22b9df36ae735185c4d19e174735bd47ec7b3faeb" "basis" 1412 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/state.json" "file_sha256" "39ca3612cc1476f345b5b0c22b9df36ae735185c4d19e174735bd47ec7b3faeb" "basis" 1412 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left of ${a}_{33}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-3{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-4{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-3{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-4{C}_{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/state.json" "file_sha256" "39ca3612cc1476f345b5b0c22b9df36ae735185c4d19e174735bd47ec7b3faeb" "basis" 1412 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9621d434515595164b9a7b82a0b62acb60abf05f431523c0f0f85bd07935f033" "basis" 1412 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42917" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/state.json" "file_sha256" "39ca3612cc1476f345b5b0c22b9df36ae735185c4d19e174735bd47ec7b3faeb" "basis" 1412 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form $R_i:=R_i+kR_1,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/example-8050.json" "file_sha256" "a74ab800ba5388259fc34381f6d692851e5e12d8803068d7f577dced585a7dd6" "basis" 1412 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries below $a_{11}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 10 & -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $R_2:=R_2-2R_1\\quad$ and $\\quad R_3:=R_3+3R_1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/example-8050.json" "file_sha256" "a74ab800ba5388259fc34381f6d692851e5e12d8803068d7f577dced585a7dd6" "basis" 1412 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations \n\n- $R_1 := \\ldots$\n\n- $R_3:=R_3-3R_2$\n\n- $R_4:= \\ldots$\n\neach of the form $R_i:=R_i+kR_2,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary row operations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/example-8048.json" "file_sha256" "3d06a1f7123d23ae1e8c213fb6ed49406529987e39bbdeb0b7afedbc75f9f8aa" "basis" 1412 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries above and below $a_{22}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing elementary row operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $R_1 := R_1 - 2R_2\\quad$ and $\\quad R_4:=R_4+2R_2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/example-8048.json" "file_sha256" "3d06a1f7123d23ae1e8c213fb6ed49406529987e39bbdeb0b7afedbc75f9f8aa" "basis" 1412 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8051" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 7 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form $C_i:=C_i+kC_3,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/example-8051.json" "file_sha256" "b98fe5652b8d5deb7aac1fb0b91514ba8e467efd28b6b2a57154d62d802a1937" "basis" 1412 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8051" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left of $a_{13}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 7 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+6{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -16 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+5{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -16 & -17 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $C_1:=C_1+6C_3\\quad$ and $\\quad C_2:=C_2+5C_3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/example-8051.json" "file_sha256" "b98fe5652b8d5deb7aac1fb0b91514ba8e467efd28b6b2a57154d62d802a1937" "basis" 1412 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 & -6 \\\\ 7 & -2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & -3 & -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations \n\n- $C_1:=\\ldots$\n\n- $C_3:= C_3 - 4C_2$\n\n- $C_4:= \\ldots$\n\neach of the form $C_i:=C_i+kC_2,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary column operations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/example-8049.json" "file_sha256" "514e03e9fd86289952c702a8d0bc7bb0549142370c2736e2297bccb40ba4839f" "basis" 1412 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left and to the right of $a_{32}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 & -6 \\\\ 7 & -2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & -3 & -6 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+5{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -3 & -1 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -23 & -3 & -6 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-4{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -3 & 11 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -23 & -3 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}-2{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -3 & 11 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -23 & -3 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $C_1:=C_1+5C_2\\quad$ and $\\quad C_4:=C_4-2C_2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/example-8049.json" "file_sha256" "514e03e9fd86289952c702a8d0bc7bb0549142370c2736e2297bccb40ba4839f" "basis" 1412 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040091/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-91160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -8 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 18 & 0 \\\\ -13 & 28 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071806/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee57a6ecf8cbd0b21df5f6a02c77afeb211071894ee8b071292695d463f280be"} {"question" "q-91160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries below ${a}_{13}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -8 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-5{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 18 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -8 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-9{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 18 & 0 \\\\ -13 & 28 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-5{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-9{R}_{1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071806/state.json" "file_sha256" "ee57a6ecf8cbd0b21df5f6a02c77afeb211071894ee8b071292695d463f280be"} {"question" "q-91160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071806/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5246ce69646f54fde2612ae5c1932087252ff7d55f96a6e822db8639d944be9b"} {"question" "q-91160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071806/state.js